WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:06.980
Thank you.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.880
And my co-presenter as
well, John, is here.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.460
He will not be presenting because.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.890
He can't be bothered, I guess.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:17.670
Pardon?

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.020
is this better?

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.270
Yeah he works for the Department
of Ed, and we work together in.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.260
John works for the
Department of Education

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.380
and we work together
in Chi Square lab.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.830
So that's about us.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.350
So I'm going to set the context.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.130
And by the way, this talk isn't
actually I teach data science,

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.720
but this talk is not about
teaching data science.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.830
It's about using data
science to teach.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.470
That's sort of the
essence of what

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.890
we do in learning analytics.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.770
We collect data from
educational interactions

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.290
and then we analyze that
and try to provide feedback

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.269
to instructors.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.270
And the story here
is, as we all know,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.800
there was a pandemic
in Argentina.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.750
We went into full
lockdown in March of 2020,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.450
and then that meant
that all instructions

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.745
needed to be online in a hurry.

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:21.370
Since we're in the
Southern hemisphere,

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.882
this actually
coincided pretty much

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:24.340
with the beginning
of the semester,

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.250
so we didn't have to
do it mid-semester.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.040
But what happened is
that everyone started

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.690
to teach using Zoom or any
kind of video conferencing

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.200
system that was
available, and we

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.530
found that less than half
the students actually

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.100
had access to a laptop.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.380
So in practice, they were
going to follow the course

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.870
on a device on an
Android forum, typically.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:59.990
And the so what we wanted
to do and this is something

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.500
that we worked on
before, is to help

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.660
them create, help instructors
create a feedback

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:09.889
loop because as Kelly
mentioned in her talk,

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:12.380
there is a lot of
nonverbal communication

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.730
that goes on when you
actually meet face to face.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.390
That's part of the
reason we're all here.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.630
And you lose that.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.978
You lose some of
it, at least when

00:02:20.978 --> 00:02:22.520
you do video
conferencing, especially

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.812
if the class is a big
class with 100 students

00:02:24.812 --> 00:02:25.770
or something like that.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.930
So what we set out to do
was to mitigate some of this

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.260
by creating a tool
to provide feedback.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.720
So the students could provide
feedback to the instructor.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.750
And we did three
iterations of this.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.870
One was slapped
together very quickly

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:50.030
because we were in a hurry
and I think less than a week.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.090
And then we did, called
out the emergency alpha

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:59.330
and then we did a proper
alpha version in April.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.230
And then we did what I
would call a beta version

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.000
in August of 2021.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.680
So for the start of the, that
semester and the first one

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.780
we did in Shiny using a
package called Shiny mobile,

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.130
and then we hooked
it up to a back end.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:24.290
And we, we have a package
called cognitoR

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.260
that allows for password
protection of Shiny apps

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.610
that we used.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.250
It's available on CRAN
if you want to use it,

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:36.130
and then we distributed
that as a PWI.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.000
And this is what it looks like.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.990
So essentially the
two on the left there,

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.700
the two icons are just links.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.660
to ah, Shiny apps, one
for the instructor and one

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.600
for the student and
they have a login

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.540
and that's handled by Amazon.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.900
And then we have a
really easy questionnaire

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.570
with some, some questions.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.400
One of them was
about connectivity

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.350
because it turned out
to be important in this

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:09.200
context and then there's two
open ended questions there.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.950
You know, what did you
learn in this class

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.800
and what would you
like to have reviewed?

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.920
And this goes back
and gets analyzed,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.930
and we use R for that.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:23.910
And then we provide feedback
to the instructor on, you know,

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:27.300
the student feedback is
summarized and provided back

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.440
to the instructor.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.370
And this is the same
package Shiny mobile

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:37.320
it provides, provides
the interface items that

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.970
look look good on a cell phone.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.920
So and this is a completely.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.350
You know, completely
reasonable way

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:51.210
of putting a Shiny
app on a cell phone.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:51.940
It works.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.580
It updates very quickly.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.000
It's very lightweight.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.280
It's only a link and
it's cross-platform.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.330
It works on an iPhone.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.310
It works on anything
that can show a web page.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.850
We did find, though,
some bandwidth issues

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.000
because if everyone
tries to log on to,

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.110
we publish this to shiny.io
if everyone tries to log on

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.670
at the same time to
provide feedback,

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.140
it actually crashes the server.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:20.790
Now you can solve
that by, you know,

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.290
by throwing hardware at it,
but that's not really scalable.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:28.290
So and, you know, I was
also a little uncomfortable

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:29.620
that this is not a native app.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.560
You can't send people to
the Play Store or App Store

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.290
to get it.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:39.090
So the second version,
we used a template

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.880
from the Android community
and there's several

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.880
of them floating around.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.270
There's one that we have that
we used that you can see there

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.650
on the slide.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:56.660
It's essentially a wrapper
that displays a web page,

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.870
but this one goes
on the Play Store.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:05.080
And, you know, you need
to know a little bit of Java.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.990
And if you hate Python, you
probably hate Java as well,

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:10.270
but it's like two lines.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:13.330
So it's not that
bad and it's still

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.100
just a Shiny app in a wrapper.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.980
So it's still not
really scalable.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:19.840
And it will only work
for android, which

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.680
which is a slight drawback.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.260
So this is a slide from one
of the instructors,

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.150
and this was what it looks like.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.030
This is probably
a later iteration.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:38.470
So she used this to
show her students,

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.450
her students, the feedback
that she was getting from them.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:43.970
And show them that this
is actually useful.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.110
And, you know,
what did we learn?

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:52.000
And as you can see, you know,
it's summarized in not a word

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.770
cloud format because
that wouldn't really

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.540
look good in on a device.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.860
So we used some other.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.960
Other tools there.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.780
And we got research
results from this,

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:08.660
which show they were
quite encouraging.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.390
And so the scale
here is 0 to 10.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:13.340
That's how it's
graded in Argentina.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:17.690
Anything below, so 4 is
the lowest passing grade.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.950
So that part of the scale
isn't actually being used.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:23.400
And so, you know, the.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.760
Distributions are not normal.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.760
And there were some
outliers like you could see.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.680
So we, you know, we use
Kendall's for correlations

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.570
and then we use
ordinal regression

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.670
instead of linear regression.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:39.780
And we found during simulation
that we could actually predict.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.870
This is post hoc though,
but we had predictive power.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:44.940
After just three weeks,
we could pretty much

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.300
tell which students were
going to finish the course.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.950
And we got good feedback
from the instructors.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.390
They said, you know,
this is actually useful.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:55.650
Useful for us.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.870
There are some issues.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:02.820
Some of them are actually
mentioned in the last talk.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.770
There's a lot of
linguistic models

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:08.350
that you can get access
to, but they are.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.920
Typically either too
general or too specific

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:14.150
when you're teaching a class.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:18.220
And the example here is
class in linguistics,

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:24.610
introductory linguistics, it's
often very specific vocabulary

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.980
that you have to learn
and that will always

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:32.110
sort of mess up the, the,
the statistical models

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.360
that have been trained
on, you know, a billion,

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.549
billion words
because this is you know,

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:39.130
these are words that you use
in two papers in your life,

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:41.590
and then you don't repeat them.

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:44.890
And then there is the issue
of Spanish versus English

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:48.640
that we actually saw
some in the last post.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.710
We did most of this
in Spanish, obviously,

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:54.130
but we are also working
on an English language

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.260
version of the same.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.160
And also, you know,
the modeling tools

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.440
and the tools for doing NLP
tend to be Anglo centric.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.520
So so that became a bit
of an issue as well.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.580
But I think we resolved.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:11.570
Most of them.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:16.700
And then we get to the beta
version, the one that's

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:20.210
out now, and that's
a true native app

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:23.300
in a framework called Flutter.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.390
The programming
language is called Dart.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:32.330
So we've sort of we've moved
the back end to the Google Cloud

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:36.680
Firebase system, and
now authentication

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.210
is done by Google.

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:43.100
And we have all the analysis
done by R with a Plumber API

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:47.730
for, you know, so that
the cell phone will.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.730
Get its information from.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:56.390
And then we have enhanced
content recommendations

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:58.910
because one of the
features of this system

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:02.780
is that if I'm
teaching statistics

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:05.510
and no one got the
standard error,

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:07.080
I may repeat that in class.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.870
But if two people did not get
what a standard deviation is,

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.560
I may not want to waste
class time on that

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.990
and I can send them
over to, you know,

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:20.090
watch a video or something.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:23.030
And we try to automate
some of that, which is

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:25.170
interesting for large classes.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:31.100
So and then we did an
email interface as well.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.265
By the way, this is a.

00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:34.390
This is what it looks like.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:37.977
Now you sign up with a QR
code, which is convenient.

00:10:37.977 --> 00:10:40.560
If you're teaching online, you
can show the code on the screen

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.215
and they can copy it right away.

00:10:43.215 --> 00:10:45.090
It's a little more
awkward if you're actually

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:48.450
watching it on a cell phone.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.680
But, you know, it's also
sign on with one link

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.380
and it's authenticated
by Google and all.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.900
And then we did
we used blastula

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.260
to send out an email
report to the instructor.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:04.210
And this was also.

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:07.120
You know, sort of
a way to get over

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.960
what I perceive to be
dashboard fatigue, that people

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.160
don't actually go and
log into the dashboards.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:16.510
They, you know, but if you
get it pushed into your email,

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.990
maybe you actually
consume the information.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:23.470
But that turned out
not to be the case.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:27.160
Once we did this,
we got this feedback

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.550
from one of the instructors.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:34.120
And Cecilia and I are married.

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:40.960
So so this was not
this was a request that

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:44.120
was difficult to ignore.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.500
And in fact, it turned
out to be impossible.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.340
So we we're sort of full circle.

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:55.960
We came back to Shiny
again, hoping that at least we

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:00.190
have the instructors on Shiny,
which would be a small, smaller

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:00.920
percentage.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:03.900
So perhaps this
is still scalable.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:08.350
It would be a lot worse if the
instructor and 200 students

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:11.730
would have to access the
server at the same time.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:15.850
So so this is an
example from her course.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:21.960
And this is also an example
from the same dashboard.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:22.590
See here.

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.510
So these are some
content recommendations

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:27.750
that the system came
up with for the course

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.880
that I've sort of assigned this
to these specific students.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:33.240
That's an artificial
intelligence piece

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.120
that we are working on.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.100
And that's what I had for you.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:39.510
This is our contact info.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:43.300
We're happy to answer
questions, both of us.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.470
So thank you.

